 "\n\t\t\t\t"typed at the previous prompt, and press Enter.\n\n', 828), ('=== Replacement ===\n\n ', 829), ('Type the characters that should replace what you ', 830), ('typed at the previous prompt, and press Enter.\n\n', 831), (' The following function keys "\n\t\t\t\t"are available at this prompt:\n\n', 832), (' The following function keys ', 833), ('are available at this prompt:\n\n', 834), ('Go To Line Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"Enter the line number that you wish to go to and hit "\n\t\t\t\t"Enter.  If there are fewer lines of text than the "\n\t\t\t\t"number you entered, you will be brought to the last "\n\t\t\t\t"line of the file.\n\n The following function keys are "\n\t\t\t\t"available in Go To Line mode:\n\n', 835), ('Go To Line Help Text\n\n ', 836), ('Enter the line number that you wish to go to and hit ', 837), ('Enter.  If there are fewer lines of text than the ', 838), ('number you entered, you will be brought to the last ', 839), ('line of the file.\n\n The following function keys are ', 840), ('available in Go To Line mode:\n\n', 841), ('Insert File Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"Type in the name of a file to be inserted into the "\n\t\t\t\t"current file buffer at the current cursor "\n\t\t\t\t"location.\n\n If you have compiled nano with multiple "\n\t\t\t\t"file buffer support, and enable multiple file buffers "\n\t\t\t\t"with the -F or --multibuffer command line flags, the "\n\t\t\t\t"Meta-F toggle, or a nanorc file, inserting a file "\n\t\t\t\t"will cause it to be loaded into a separate buffer "\n\t\t\t\t"(use Meta-< and > to switch between file buffers).  ', 842), ('Insert File Help Text\n\n ', 843), ('Type in the name of a file to be inserted into the ', 844), ('current file buffer at the current cursor ', 845), ('location.\n\n If you have compiled nano with multiple ', 846), ('file buffer support, and enable multiple file buffers ', 847), ('with the -F or --multibuffer command line flags, the ', 848), ('Meta-F toggle, or a nanorc file, inserting a file ', 849), ('will cause it to be loaded into a separate buffer ', 850), ('(use Meta-< and > to switch between file buffers).  ', 851), ('If you need another blank buffer, do not enter "\n\t\t\t\t"any filename, or type in a nonexistent filename at "\n\t\t\t\t"the prompt and press Enter.\n\n The following "\n\t\t\t\t"function keys are available in Insert File mode:\n\n', 852), ('If you need another blank buffer, do not enter ', 853), ('any filename, or type in a nonexistent filename at ', 854), ('the prompt and press Enter.\n\n The following ', 855), ('function keys are available in Insert File mode:\n\n', 856), ('Write File Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"Type the name that you wish to save the current file "\n\t\t\t\t"as and press Enter to save the file.\n\n If you have "\n\t\t\t\t"selected text with the mark, you will be prompted to "\n\t\t\t\t"save only the selected portion to a separate file.  To "\n\t\t\t\t"reduce the chance of overwriting the current file with "\n\t\t\t\t"just a portion of it, the current filename is not the "\n\t\t\t\t"default in this mode.\n\n The following function keys "\n\t\t\t\t"are available in Write File mode:\n\n', 857), ('Write File Help Text\n\n ', 858), ('Type the name that you wish to save the current file ', 859), ('as and press Enter to save the file.\n\n If you have ', 860), ('selected text with the mark, you will be prompted to ', 861), ('save only the selected portion to a separate file.  To ', 862), ('reduce the chance of overwriting the current file with ', 863), ('just a portion of it, the current filename is not the ', 864), ('default in this mode.\n\n The following function keys ', 865), ('are available in Write File mode:\n\n', 866), ('File Browser Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"The file browser is used to visually browse the "\n\t\t\t\t"directory structure to select a file for reading "\n\t\t\t\t"or writing.  You may use the arrow keys or Page Up/"\n\t\t\t\t"Down to browse through the files, and S or Enter to "\n\t\t\t\t"choose the selected file or enter the selected "\n\t\t\t\t"directory.  To move up one level, select the "\n\t\t\t\t"directory called ".." at the top of the file "\n\t\t\t\t"list.\n\n The following function keys are available "\n\t\t\t\t"in the file browser:\n\n', 867), ('File Browser Help Text\n\n ', 868), ('The file browser is used to visually browse the ', 869), ('directory structure to select a file for reading ', 870), ('or writing.  You may use the arrow keys or Page Up/', 871), ('Down to browse through the files, and S or Enter to ', 872), ('choose the selected file or enter the selected ', 873), ('directory.  To move up one level, select the ', 874), ('directory called ".." at the top of the file ', 875), ('list.\n\n The following function keys are available ', 876), ('in the file browser:\n\n', 877), ('Browser Search Command Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"Enter the words or characters you would like to "\n\t\t\t\t"search for, and then press Enter.  If there is a "\n\t\t\t\t"match for the text you entered, the screen will be "\n\t\t\t\t"updated to the location of the nearest match for the "\n\t\t\t\t"search string.\n\n The previous search string will be "\n\t\t\t\t"shown in brackets after the search prompt.  Hitting "\n\t\t\t\t"Enter without entering any text will perform the "\n\t\t\t\t"previous search.\n\n', 878), ('Browser Search Command Help Text\n\n ', 879), ('Enter the words or characters you would like to ', 880), ('search for, and then press Enter.  If there is a ', 881), ('match for the text you entered, the screen will be ', 882), ('updated to the location of the nearest match for the ', 883), ('search string.\n\n The previous search string will be ', 884), ('shown in brackets after the search prompt.  Hitting ', 885), ('Enter without entering any text will perform the ', 886), ('previous search.\n\n', 887), (' The following function keys "\n\t\t\t\t"are available at this prompt:\n\n', 888), (' The following function keys ', 889), ('are available at this prompt:\n\n', 890), ('Browser Go To Directory Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"Enter the name of the directory you would like to "\n\t\t\t\t"browse to.\n\n If tab completion has not been "\n\t\t\t\t"disabled, you can use the Tab key to (attempt to) "\n\t\t\t\t"automatically complete the directory name.\n\n The "\n\t\t\t\t"following function keys are available in Browser Go "\n\t\t\t\t"To Directory mode:\n\n', 891), ('Browser Go To Directory Help Text\n\n ', 892), ('Enter the name of the directory you would like to ', 893), ('browse to.\n\n If tab completion has not been ', 894), ('disabled, you can use the Tab key to (attempt to) ', 895), ('automatically complete the directory name.\n\n The ', 896), ('following function keys are available in Browser Go ', 897), ('To Directory mode:\n\n', 898), ('Spell Check Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"The spell checker checks the spelling of all text in "\n\t\t\t\t"the current file.  When an unknown word is "\n\t\t\t\t"encountered, it is highlighted and a replacement can "\n\t\t\t\t"be edited.  It will then prompt to replace every "\n\t\t\t\t"instance of the given misspelled word in the current "\n\t\t\t\t"file, or, if you have selected text with the mark, in "\n\t\t\t\t"the selected text.\n\n The following function keys "\n\t\t\t\t"are available in Spell Check mode:\n\n', 899), ('Spell Check Help Text\n\n ', 900), ('The spell checker checks the spelling of all text in ', 901), ('the current file.  When an unknown word is ', 902), ('encountered, it is highlighted and a replacement can ', 903), ('be edited.  It will then prompt to replace every ', 904), ('instance of the given misspelled word in the current ', 905), ('file, or, if you have selected text with the mark, in ', 906), ('the selected text.\n\n The following function keys ', 907), ('are available in Spell Check mode:\n\n', 908), ('Execute Command Help Text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"This mode allows you to insert the output of a "\n\t\t\t\t"command run by the shell into the current buffer (or "\n\t\t\t\t"into a new buffer).  If the command is preceded by \'|\' "\n\t\t\t\t"(the pipe symbol), the current contents of the buffer "\n\t\t\t\t"(or marked region) will be piped to the command.  ', 909), ('Execute Command Help Text\n\n ', 910), ('This mode allows you to insert the output of a ', 911), ('command run by the shell into the current buffer (or ', 912), ("into a new buffer).  If the command is preceded by '|' ", 913), ('(the pipe symbol), the current contents of the buffer ', 914), ('(or marked region) will be piped to the command.  ', 915), ('If you just need another blank buffer, do not enter any "\n\t\t\t\t"command.\n\n You can also pick one of four tools, or cut a "\n\t\t\t\t"large piece of the buffer, or put the editor to sleep.\n\n', 916), ('If you just need another blank buffer, do not enter any ', 917), ('command.\n\n You can also pick one of four tools, or cut a ', 918), ('large piece of the buffer, or put the editor to sleep.\n\n', 919), (' The following function keys "\n\t\t\t\t"are available at this prompt:\n\n', 920), (' The following function keys ', 921), ('are available at this prompt:\n\n', 922), ('=== Linter ===\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"In this mode, the status bar shows an error message or "\n\t\t\t\t"warning, and the cursor is put at the corresponding "\n\t\t\t\t"position in the file.  With PageUp and PageDown you "\n\t\t\t\t"can switch to earlier and later messages.\n\n', 923), ('=== Linter ===\n\n ', 924), ('In this mode, the status bar shows an error message or ', 925), ('warning, and the cursor is put at the corresponding ', 926), ('position in the file.  With PageUp and PageDown you ', 927), ('can switch to earlier and later messages.\n\n', 928), (' The following function keys are "\n\t\t\t\t"available in Linter mode:\n\n', 929), (' The following function keys are ', 930), ('available in Linter mode:\n\n', 931), ('Main nano help text\n\n "\n\t\t\t\t"The nano editor is designed to emulate the "\n\t\t\t\t"functionality and ease-of-use of the UW Pico text "\n\t\t\t\t"editor.  There are four main sections of the editor.  "\n\t\t\t\t"The top line shows the program version, the current "\n\t\t\t\t"filename being edited, and whether or not the file "\n\t\t\t\t"has been modified.  Next is the main editor window "\n\t\t\t\t"showing the file being edited.  The status line is "\n\t\t\t\t"the third line from the bottom and shows important "\n\t\t\t\t"messages.  ', 932), ('Main nano help text\n\n ', 933), ('The nano editor is designed to emulate the ', 934), ('functionality and ease-of-use of the UW Pico text ', 935), ('editor.  There are four main sections of the editor.  ', 936), ('The top line shows the program version, the current ', 937), ('filename being edited, and whether or not the file ', 938), ('has been modified.  Next is the main editor window ', 939), ('showing the file being edited.  The status line is ', 940), ('the third line from the bottom and shows important ', 941), ('messages.  ', 942), ('The bottom two lines show the most commonly used "\n\t\t\t\t"shortcuts in the editor.\n\n Shortcuts are written as "\n\t\t\t\t"follows: Control-key sequences are notated with a \'^\' "\n\t\t\t\t"and can be entered either by using the Ctrl key or "\n\t\t\t\t"pressing the Esc key twice.  Meta-key sequences are "\n\t\t\t\t"notated with \'M-\' and can be entered using either the "\n\t\t\t\t"Alt, Cmd, or Esc key, depending on your keyboard setup.  ', 943), ('The bottom two lines show the most commonly used ', 944), ('shortcuts in the editor.\n\n Shortcuts are written as ', 945), ("follows: Control-key sequences are notated with a '^' ", 946), ('and can be entered either by using the Ctrl key or ', 947), ('pressing the Esc key twice.  Meta-key sequences are ', 948), ("notated with 'M-' and can be entered using either the ", 949), ('Alt, Cmd, or Esc key, depending on your keyboard setup.  ', 950), ('Also, pressing Esc twice and then typing a "\n\t\t\t\t"three-digit decimal number from 000 to 255 will enter "\n\t\t\t\